# Caddyfile — TLS ingress for the dsh-gateway tunnel.
#
# Rendered by deploy/vps/install.sh into /etc/caddy/Caddyfile (mode 0644: it
# holds no secrets). Placeholder values are substituted by that script, and the
# installer refuses to install a file with an unresolved token left in it —
# every placeholder below, the ones in comments included, is substituted.
#
# Shape of the path a phone request takes:
#
#   phone ──HTTPS──> Caddy ──HTTP, loopback──> frps 127.0.0.1:18787
#                       │                            ║ frp tunnel (TLS,
#                       │                            ║ encrypted, token)
#                       └── this file                ▼
#                                                frpc on the Mac
#                                                   └── 127.0.0.1:8787
#
# Caddy is the only component with a public port. The gateway itself speaks
# plain HTTP and is never reachable off the Mac.
#
# Two ports are in play, both chosen by the installer:
#
#   __HTTPS_PORT__   the application, TLS-terminated.
#   __HTTP_PORT__    plain HTTP. In the default `--tls internal` mode it serves
#                   exactly one thing — the CA certificate a phone installs —
#                   and redirects everything else to HTTPS. Under
#                   `--tls acme` it is where ACME's http-01 challenge lands.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why these are not 80/443, and where the certificate comes from.
#
# Some hosting providers — mainland Chinese clouds most notably — block inbound
# 80 and 443 until the domain has an ICP filing, and block the usual alternative
# HTTP ports (2053, 2083, 2087, 2096, 8880, 9443) along with them. High ports
# are usually left alone, which is why 8080 and 8443 are the shipped default —
# but that is a guess about your provider, not a property of this software. If
# your host carries 80 and 443, use --tls acme --https-port 443 --http-port 80
# and none of the below applies to you.
#
# A publicly trusted certificate is then out of reach, because every ACME
# challenge that does not need a DNS provider API needs one of those two ports:
#
#   http-01       port 80
#   tls-alpn-01   port 443
#   dns-01        a DNS provider API, which sslip.io does not offer
#
# So the default `--tls internal` has Caddy sign with a CA it generates on this
# host, and publishes that root on the plain-HTTP port for the phone to install
# once. Everything else — the PWA, its service worker, Secure cookies, the
# WebSocket — then behaves exactly as it would under a publicly trusted
# certificate.
#
# `--tls acme` renders the other variant instead: no internal CA and no CA site,
# a publicly trusted certificate obtained over ACME, which requires the
# challenge ports to be reachable (normally --https-port 443 --http-port 80).
# The installer warns if acme is selected with any other pair.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Global options. Automatic HTTPS is already on (each site address is a
# hostname), so this block is optional — uncomment a line if you want it. Under
# `--tls acme`, while testing issuance, use the staging CA to avoid burning the
# production rate limit (5 failed validations per account, per hostname, per
# hour); remember to switch back afterwards, or the phone will not trust the
# certificate:
# {
#	email you@example.com
#	acme_ca https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
# }

# The application. __DOMAIN__ is the sslip.io name that resolves to this VPS's
# IP.
__DOMAIN__:__HTTPS_PORT__ {
__TLS_DIRECTIVE__

	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	# HSTS. Only sent on HTTPS. One year, and includeSubDomains is safe here
	# because this name is a single host under a shared wildcard zone that we
	# do not otherwise use — if you later serve other sslip.io names from
	# this host, narrow or drop this header rather than poisoning them.
	#
	# `preload` is deliberately absent: submitting to the browser preload
	# lists is effectively irreversible and would be a poor fit for a
	# deployment whose domain is generated from an IP address.
	#
	# Note what HSTS means for the CA site on the plain-HTTP port: browsers
	# apply an HSTS policy to the host, ignoring the port, so a browser that
	# has once loaded this app over HTTPS will upgrade a later
	# http://__DOMAIN__:__HTTP_PORT__/ca.crt to https on that port, where
	# Caddy speaks plain HTTP. Install or re-fetch the CA before the first
	# HTTPS visit, from a browser that has not seen this header, or after
	# clearing the host's HSTS state.
	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
	}

	# WebSockets need nothing special: Caddy detects the Upgrade header and
	# proxies the connection (that is how GET /api/v1/events works). It does
	# not apply default proxy read/write timeouts either, which is what lets a
	# long agent turn stay open. If you ever add timeouts, keep them above the
	# gateway's session.idleTimeout (5m by default) or idle streams get cut.
__CA_ROUTE__
	# The application itself. Two `handle` blocks at the same level are mutually
	# exclusive: the first matching one runs, so /ca.crt above never reaches the
	# proxy, and everything else never touches the certificate file.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:18787 {
			# Disable response buffering so tokens reach the phone as the
			# agent emits them instead of arriving in one lump at the end of a
			# turn.
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}

	# No `encode` directive on purpose: the payloads are small JSON frames and
	# already-compressed assets, and transparent compression in front of a
	# streaming/WebSocket endpoint is a well-known way to introduce buffering
	# bugs for no real gain.

	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Optional: restrict by client IP.
	#
	# Replace the fallback handle below with the guarded pair here. A /ca.crt
	# handle above, where one is present (the default `--tls internal` mode),
	# stays outside the allowlist on purpose: a phone needs the root before it
	# can use anything else, and a root certificate is public information.
	#
	# Be aware of what an allowlist buys: mobile carriers rotate addresses
	# constantly and put many subscribers behind CGNAT, so it both locks you
	# out mid-session and rarely excludes a determined attacker. It is defence
	# in depth, not a substitute for pairing and revoking devices.
	#
	#	@phone remote_ip 203.0.113.0/24 198.51.100.7
	#	handle @phone {
	#		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:18787 {
	#			flush_interval -1
	#		}
	#	}
	#	handle {
	#		respond "not found" 404
	#	}
	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
}

# Plain HTTP. Anything arriving here — the bare IP, a stale bookmark, a probe —
# is sent to the canonical HTTPS name rather than served in the clear, so no
# request is answered without TLS. (The one exception, in the default mode, is
# the CA certificate a phone has to install before it can trust anything; a root
# certificate is public information.) Caddy answers ACME HTTP-01 challenges
# internally before it reaches these routes, so this catch-all does not interfere
# with issuance for __DOMAIN__.
#
# This port is the *fallback* way to fetch the root, not the primary one. It is
# plain HTTP, so a provider that filters unfiled hostnames can answer it with an
# ICP block page instead — keyed on the Host header being a domain and not on the
# IP, so it is not something this file can work around. The copy served over
# HTTPS by the site above is what the documentation tells a phone to use.
:__HTTP_PORT__ {
__CA_HANDLE__

	# Everything else goes to HTTPS. There is no redirect to preserve on the
	# port ACME would have used, so this is a plain convenience.
	handle {
		redir https://__DOMAIN__:__HTTPS_PORT__{uri} permanent
	}
}
